<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The parties married in 1995 and have two sons. The marriage ran into the familiar sequence of cross-proceedings: the wife&rsquo;s complaint under Section 498A IPC (settled through JHALSA-mediated terms in connected anticipatory bail proceedings), her maintenance petition at Dhanbad under Section 125 CrPC, and the husband&rsquo;s restitution suit, which produced an ex parte decree on 11 November 2016. When cohabitation did not resume, the husband filed for divorce in April 2019 under Section 13(1)(1-A)(ii) HMA, relying on the wife&rsquo;s non-compliance with the restitution decree. The Family Court dismissed the suit in March 2025.</p>

<h2>What the Division Bench held</h2>
<p>The appeal failed on the facts and on the standard of review. On the facts: &ldquo;the appellant has not adduced any cogent and reliable evidence that the respondent-wife had not resumed&rdquo; conjugal life (para 61), and the evidence &ldquo;does not disclose the existence of animus deserendi&rdquo; on her part (para 62). On the standard: &ldquo;Perversity is a high threshold for intervention, distinguishing it from a mere error of fact or law&rdquo; (para 33), and the Family Court&rsquo;s appraisal crossed no such threshold (para 63). The appeal was dismissed (para 64), with pending applications disposed of.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The conduct finding:</strong> the husband — employed with the Railways — commenced maintenance payments only after his revision against the 10 June 2016 Dhanbad order was dismissed in July 2020. The Bench read this as reflecting a &ldquo;lack of concern towards the maintenance and educational needs of his wife and children&rdquo; (para 52). In a 13(1A) case, where the petitioner effectively pleads the breakdown that followed his own decree, such findings corrode the claim.</p>
</div>

<h2>Section 13(1A)(ii): right, but not self-executing</h2>
<p>Section 13(1A)(ii) HMA entitles either party to seek divorce where restitution has not occurred for a year after the decree. Two misconceptions recur in practice. First, that the decree itself proves non-resumption — it does not; the petitioner must prove the fact of non-restitution and that it is not the product of his own refusal or obstruction. Second, that an ex parte decree is as good as a contested one for this purpose — formally it is, but courts scrutinise what the decree-holder actually did afterwards: letters, visits, attempts at mediation, payment of maintenance, provision for children. A decree-holder who obtained his decree in the respondent&rsquo;s absence, paid maintenance only under compulsion, and then presented the lapse of time as desertion will meet exactly the reception this appellant met.</p>

<h2>The appellate lens</h2>
<p>The Bench grounded its approach in the settled first-appeal framework — <em>Jagdish Singh v. Madhuri Devi</em>, (2008) 10 SCC 497, and allied authority — under which findings of fact by the Family Court are respected unless shown perverse. Matrimonial appeals are not re-trials; the appellant&rsquo;s task is to identify a finding no reasonable court could have reached, and conflicting testimony about dates of separation and payment quantum (of which this record had its share) is precisely the material on which the trial court&rsquo;s assessment prevails.</p>

<h2>Takeaways</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Build the post-decree record: a restitution decree begins, not ends, the evidentiary task for a later 13(1A)(ii) petition.</li>
  <li>Maintenance conduct is read as character evidence of matrimonial sincerity — arrears and resisted orders will be quoted back.</li>
  <li>Delay between decree and divorce petition (here over two and a half years) invites the question of what the petitioner did in the interval.</li>
  <li>Frame first appeals around perversity, with specific findings targeted — generalised re-argument of the evidence fails.</li>
</ul>
</div>
